WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.445
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.445 --> 00:00:09.438
I was sitting on the edge of the boat,
my back against the water.

00:00:09.438 --> 00:00:13.480
Mask, check, fins, check,

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.837
air pressure, 3,000 psi.

00:00:17.837 --> 00:00:21.201
I grabbed my camera and
started the countdown.

00:00:21.201 --> 00:00:27.556
Three, two, one,
I rolled back and hit the water.

00:00:27.556 --> 00:00:31.895
As the bubbles settled and
the visibility cleared, I felt it.

00:00:31.895 --> 00:00:36.424
This could be the dive
I've always dreamed of.

00:00:36.424 --> 00:00:42.785
As I descended, I was surrounded by
over 70 sharks from different species.

00:00:42.785 --> 00:00:47.064
We reached the sandy floor and
formed a semi-circle.

00:00:47.064 --> 00:00:49.331
My mum was to my left.

00:00:49.331 --> 00:00:53.015
I started shooting away, my favorite.

00:00:53.015 --> 00:00:56.399
A beautiful lemon shark arrived.

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:03.634
Finally, 20 minutes into the dive,
the thing I've been waiting.

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:10.091
Two massive tiger sharks, one of them
measuring over 15 feet in length.

00:01:12.026 --> 00:01:15.674
They started circling the group and

00:01:15.674 --> 00:01:19.968
getting close, Very close.

00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:24.418
As the larger of the two sharks,
swam past me,

00:01:24.418 --> 00:01:29.097
heading towards my mum,
I saw it sigh then wipe,

00:01:29.097 --> 00:01:32.996
something they do right before biting.

00:01:32.996 --> 00:01:37.007
The shark suddenly turned and
smacked me with its tail.

00:01:37.007 --> 00:01:41.198
I lost my balance, but as soon as I
regained it, I clipped the shutter and

00:01:41.198 --> 00:01:43.622
saw the scariest image I've ever taken.

00:01:48.404 --> 00:01:51.937
The shark took a bite of my mom.

00:01:51.937 --> 00:01:57.349
Luckily, she had her camera with her and
was able to use it as a shield.

00:01:57.349 --> 00:02:01.059
But the shark keep biting,
pushing her further and

00:02:01.059 --> 00:02:04.258
further away from
the group with each bite.

00:02:04.258 --> 00:02:08.017
Finally, it let go.

00:02:11.112 --> 00:02:13.640
Just when I thought we were safe,
the shark turned and

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.221
this time headed straight at me.

00:02:15.221 --> 00:02:18.543
The same thing happened,

00:02:18.543 --> 00:02:23.678
it sigh then wipe, and a bite followed.

00:02:23.678 --> 00:02:28.818
After this, we decided to abort the dive.

00:02:28.818 --> 00:02:36.963
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:36.963 --> 00:02:39.821
&gt;&gt; Once we reached the surface, and

00:02:39.821 --> 00:02:45.449
the fear and adrenaline faded,
my head flooded with questions.

00:02:45.449 --> 00:02:48.393
Why did the dive go so
wrong, who was at fault?

00:02:48.393 --> 00:02:54.508
In hindsight, we changed the behavior
of the shark by chumming the water,

00:02:54.508 --> 00:02:58.696
which is putting dead bait
in a cage to lure them in.

00:02:58.696 --> 00:03:05.724
The shark was acting normal, it was
looking for the food they were sensing.

00:03:05.724 --> 00:03:07.998
In fact, it was even being gentle.

00:03:07.998 --> 00:03:13.718
Remember, this animals weigh 1,500 pounds,
have teeth,

00:03:13.718 --> 00:03:19.445
as sharp as racers that can crush
a turtle shell, just like that.

00:03:19.445 --> 00:03:22.473
Yet none of us had a scratch, not my mum,

00:03:22.473 --> 00:03:27.302
not my me, not even the crystal
dome of the camera had a scratch.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.471
This dive was a turning point for me.

00:03:33.471 --> 00:03:38.131
For the past ten years, I've been
travelling the most remote places in

00:03:38.131 --> 00:03:43.024
the world looking for animals that
we've driven to the brink of extinction

00:03:43.024 --> 00:03:46.461
all to promote conservation
through photography.

00:03:46.461 --> 00:03:51.197
My thinking was,
that if I show you the giant of the ocean,

00:03:52.792 --> 00:03:57.169
Some of it's tiniest creatures,
there's a sea urchin there,

00:03:57.169 --> 00:03:59.203
let's see if you can spot it.

00:03:59.203 --> 00:04:02.252
Or even big stars, most famous stars.

00:04:04.157 --> 00:04:07.679
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Your mind will be flooded

00:04:07.679 --> 00:04:08.288
with curiosity.

00:04:08.288 --> 00:04:13.440
And you'll want to experience and
explore the ocean.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.300
And I know deep inside that once you do,

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:21.248
you'll fall in love with it and
want to protect it, just like I did.

00:04:21.248 --> 00:04:25.774
However, I was not having
the impact I hoped for.

00:04:25.774 --> 00:04:32.477
As much as I wanted my passion and
love for the ocean to be contagious,

00:04:32.477 --> 00:04:37.332
the truth is we are very
detached from the ocean and

00:04:37.332 --> 00:04:43.125
it is very hard to protect
something that seems so foreign.

00:04:43.125 --> 00:04:48.127
For example, on the left, we have
a picture of my Labradoodle Smokey.

00:04:48.127 --> 00:04:52.073
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This handsome dog got 107 likes

00:04:52.073 --> 00:04:53.825
on Instagram.

00:04:53.825 --> 00:04:59.439
On the right,
we have a flying whale with only 44 likes.

00:04:59.439 --> 00:05:02.969
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean, Smoky is very cute I know but

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:06.452
two and
a half times as cute as a flying whale?

00:05:06.452 --> 00:05:11.774
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Moreover, after my encounter

00:05:11.774 --> 00:05:17.355
with the shark, I finally understood that
there is no image beautiful enough or

00:05:17.355 --> 00:05:22.603
story compelling enough that I can tell
you, that I can tell someone who's

00:05:22.603 --> 00:05:27.706
afraid of the ocean or afraid of sharks
that would make them want to dive.

00:05:27.706 --> 00:05:29.655
A fear I could now relate to.

00:05:35.217 --> 00:05:40.005
If I couldn't reach people's hearts,
I needed to find a better way.

00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:44.294
I came across this
organization called Oceana,

00:05:44.294 --> 00:05:48.699
whose mission is save the ocean,
feed the world.

00:05:48.699 --> 00:05:53.050
I realized that the ocean is much
more than a beautiful place.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:56.342
Today, 1 billion people
depend on the ocean,

00:05:56.342 --> 00:05:59.237
including some of the world's poorest.

00:05:59.237 --> 00:06:01.929
But we're taking much more
than the ocean can produce.

00:06:01.929 --> 00:06:06.904
And in the process,
we're destroying a very delicate balance,

00:06:06.904 --> 00:06:09.985
that took millions of years to perfect.

00:06:09.985 --> 00:06:13.971
To add to the problem,
we will soon reach 9 billion people.

00:06:13.971 --> 00:06:18.309
And we don't have the resources to feed
such a large and growing population.

00:06:20.054 --> 00:06:25.046
The ocean offers an abundant and
sustainable source of food for the world.

00:06:25.046 --> 00:06:28.775
And unlike land-based agriculture or
farming,

00:06:28.775 --> 00:06:31.802
wild fish produce no greenhouse gases.

00:06:31.802 --> 00:06:35.690
It does not consume our precious and
scarce fresh water and

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.840
does not utilize fertile land.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:41.559
But to take advantage of
this natural resource,

00:06:41.559 --> 00:06:46.465
we need to change the nature of
our relationship with the ocean.

00:06:46.465 --> 00:06:52.016
Sharks are a sign of a healthy ocean
because they protect the coral reefs

00:06:52.016 --> 00:06:58.319
by controlling the population of smaller
predator fish who feed on herbivores.

00:06:58.319 --> 00:07:01.557
Without these carnivores,
algae expands and

00:07:01.557 --> 00:07:05.218
coral can no longer compete,
killing off the reef.

00:07:05.218 --> 00:07:09.636
These reefs are critical,
they are the breathing ground for

00:07:09.636 --> 00:07:14.149
all fish species, without them,
fish population deplete.

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:19.724
Today, we kill 100
million sharks per year.

00:07:19.724 --> 00:07:23.272
That is almost 12,000 sharks per hour.

00:07:23.272 --> 00:07:28.547
Imagine the impact we are having on
the balance of marine ecosystems.

00:07:28.547 --> 00:07:33.460
We have already wiped out
over 90% of all large fish

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:36.636
species like to tunas and sharks.

00:07:36.636 --> 00:07:40.513
In contrast,
sharks skill 12 humans per year.

00:07:40.513 --> 00:07:43.967
So I ask you,
who is the deadliest predator?

00:07:43.967 --> 00:07:46.547
Who should we be afraid of?

00:07:46.547 --> 00:07:50.806
For some people,
images like the one I have shown you,

00:07:50.806 --> 00:07:53.307
awaken our sense of wonder, and

00:07:53.307 --> 00:07:59.059
make us want to explore what's 70%
of the world's surface is made of.

00:07:59.059 --> 00:08:04.155
For others not so much but
no matter what group you're in,

00:08:04.155 --> 00:08:09.043
the ocean is our most precious and
valuable resource and

00:08:09.043 --> 00:08:11.541
it holds a key to our future.

00:08:11.541 --> 00:08:13.078
So what can we do to protect it?

00:08:13.078 --> 00:08:17.977
We can follow and
support organizations like Oceana.

00:08:17.977 --> 00:08:22.216
We can elect government officials
who support good ocean policy and

00:08:22.216 --> 00:08:24.082
we can eat sustainable food.

00:08:24.082 --> 00:08:25.426
It is not too late,

00:08:25.426 --> 00:08:30.634
we can replenish fish populations in
as little as 10 years, making this

00:08:30.634 --> 00:08:35.853
goal attainable in our lifetime an issue
to push in the political agenda.

00:08:35.853 --> 00:08:39.138
Let's change how we
coexist with the ocean.

00:08:39.138 --> 00:08:42.435
And together,
let's provide 1 billion healthy and

00:08:42.435 --> 00:08:45.081
sustainable fish meals a day to the world.

00:08:45.081 --> 00:08:45.909
Thank you.

00:08:45.909 --> 00:08:48.982
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:48.982 --> 00:08:53.594
[MUSIC]

